<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Indochina War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>, after France tried to restore its rule over Indochina following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The Viet Minh, aided from 1950 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, defeated the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords then ended French Indochina and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th parallel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war.jpg" alt="First Indochina War" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohamed Ajjani · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Indochina_War_COLLAGE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> in stages between 1858 and 1887, and Vietnamese nationalism grew until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> broke French control. After the Japanese surrender in August 1945, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a> abdicated on 25 August and handed power to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, and on 2 September Ho Chi Minh proclaimed the Democratic Republic of Vietnam. Allied commanders had placed the south under British Admiral Mountbatten, and on 23 September 1945 French forces overthrew the Viet Minh administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>. A preliminary accord of March 1946 recognised the Democratic Republic as a free state within a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, but talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> broke down over independence and the reunification of Cochinchina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Full war broke out between French and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> forces in December 1946. In 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> and General Võ Nguyên Giáp withdrew into the mountainous Việt Bắc, while the French took the main towns by March. In 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> united Cochinchina, Annam and Tonkin as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>. About 325,000 of the 500,000 French troops were Indochinese, and soldiers came from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a>, since governments forbade sending metropolitan recruits.</p><p>The Chinese Communist victory of 1949 changed the war. In 1950 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> recognised the Democratic Republic, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a> and began to aid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Giáp reorganised his forces into five infantry divisions, and the conflict came to resemble a conventional war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954, the last major battle, ended in a decisive French defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Geneva Conference opened in May 1954, and the accords were signed on 21 July. They set a ceasefire, which took effect on 11 August, and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th parallel, with a free election planned for 1956 to reunify the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> undertook to respect the independence of Vietnam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>.</p><p>Estimates put military deaths at 400,000 to 842,707 and civilian deaths at 125,000 to 400,000. Both sides committed war crimes. French troops killed over 200 women and children in the Mỹ Trạch massacre of 29 November 1947, and one historian estimates that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> assassinated 100,000 to 150,000 civilians. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> leftists called it the dirty war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a> began on 1 November 1954, and an Algerian leader praised the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> as an incentive to insurrection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Indochina War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>First Indochina War</b> (1946–54) the Viet Minh controlled most rural areas while many towns fell to the French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>the First Indochina War</b> from 1946, a French Union force of up to several hundred thousand men, including Vietnamese, Laotian and Cambodian troops, fought the Viet Minh, losing the frontier posts in 1950 and suffering final defeat at Dien Bien Phu in May 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of Vietnam grew out of the State of Vietnam, created on 14 June 1949 through co-operation between anti-communist Vietnamese and the French during the <b>First Indochina War</b>, with the former emperor Bảo Đại as chief of state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
